''ger'' = Dan. ''anger'', compunction, penitence, regret; cf. OFries. ''angst'', ''ongost'' = OHG. ''angust'', MHG. ''angest'', G. ''angst'', anxiety, anguish, fear, used adjectively, anxious, afraid (> Dan. ''angst'', n., fear; adj., anxious, afraid; the Icel. ''angist'', anguish, occurring esp. in theological writers, and resting on the ult. related L. ''angustia'', > E. ''anguish'', q. v.), with different formative from the same root which appears in Icel. ''öngr'', narrow, strait, = AS. ''ange'', ''onge'', reg. with umlaut ''ænge'', ''enge'', narrow, strait, also anxious, troubled (cf. in comp. ''angsum'', narrow, strait, anxious, ''angsumnes'', and ''angnes'', anxiety; and cf. ''angnægl'', E. ''agnail'', q. v.), = OS. ''engi'' = OHG. ''angi'', ''engi'', MHG. ''enge'', G. ''eng'' = Goth. ''aggwus'', narrow, strait, = Gr. ἐγγύς, also ἄγχι, adv., near, close, = Skt. ''a<sup>n</sup>hu'', narrow, strait, √ ''a<sup>n</sup>h'', be narrow or distressing, the root appearing also in Gr. ἄγχειν = L. ''angere'', compress, strangle, choke (> L. ''angina'', compression, anxiety, ''angor'', anguish, anxiety, ''angustus'', narrow, strait, ''anxius'', anxious, etc.: see ''angor'' = ''anger''<sup>2</sup>, ''angust'', ''anguish'', ''anxious'', etc.), and being widely extended in Slavic: OBulg. ''a<sup>n</sup>zŭkŭ'', narrow, Russ. ''uzĭ'', narrow, ''uzina'', a strait, defile, etc., OBulg. ''ve<sup>n</sup>zati'' = Bohem. ''vazati'' = Russ. ''vyazatĭ'', etc., bind, tie.] '''1†.''' Grief; trouble; distress; anguish.
{{smaller block|For the deth of whiche childe the ''anger'' and sorow was muche the more.
{{right|''Caxton'', Jason, 76b. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''2.''' A revengeful passion or emotion directed against one who inflicts a real or supposed wrong; “uneasiness or discomposure of mind upon the receipt of any injury, with a present purpose of revenge,” ''Locke''; wrath; ire.
{{smaller block|While therefore the true end of sudden ''anger'' is self-defence, the true end of resentment is the execution of justice against offenders.
{{right|''H. N. Oxenham'', Short Studies, p. 40.}}
<poem>The war-storm shakes the solid hills
Beneath its tread of ''anger''. ''Whittier'', Our River.</poem>}}

'''3.''' An individual fit of anger; an expression of anger, as a threat: in this sense it may be used in the plural.
{{smaller block|<poem>Thro’ light and shadow thou dost range,
Sudden glances, sweet and strange,
Delicious spites and darling ''angers'',
And airy forms of flitting change.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Madeline.}}}}

'''4.''' Pain or smart, as of a sore or swelling. {{smaller|This sense is still retained by the adjective. See ''angry'', 8. [Obsolete or dialectal.]}}
{{smaller block|I made the experiment, setting the moxa where the first violence of my pain began, and where the greatest ''anger'' and soreness still continued. {{right|''Sir W. Temple.''}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Anger, Vexation, Indignation, Resentment, Wrath, Ire, Choler, Rage, Fury'', passion, displeasure, dudgeon, irritation, gall, bile, spleen. ''Vexation'' is the least forcible of these words, expressing the annoyance and impatient chafing of one whose mood has been crossed, whose expectations have not been realized, etc. ''Indignation'' may be the most high-minded and unselfish; it is intense feeling in view of grossly unworthy conduct, whether toward one’s self or toward others. The other words denote al-}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller|most exclusively feeling excited by the sense of personal injury. ''Anger'' is a sudden violent feeling of displeasure over injury, disobedience, etc., accompanied by a retaliatory impulse; it easily becomes excessive, and its manifestation is generally accompanied by a loss of self-control. ''Resentment'' is the broadest in its meaning, denoting the instinctive and proper recoil of feeling when one is injured, and often a deep and bitter brooding over past wrongs, with a consequent hatred and settled desire for vengeance; it is, in the latter sense, the coolest and most permanent of these feelings. ''Wrath'' and ''ire'' express sudden feeling of great power, and are often associated with the notion of the superiority of the person: as, the ''wrath'' of Jove, the ''ire'' of Achilles. They are often the result of wounded pride. ''Ire'' is poetic. ''Wrath'' has also an exalted sense, expressive of a lofty indignation visiting justice upon wrong-doing. ''Rage'' is an outburst of anger, with little or no self-control; ''fury'' is even more violent than ''rage'', rising almost to madness. The chief characteristic of ''choler'' is quickness to rise; it is irascibility, easily breaking into a high degree of resentful feeling.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>White was her cheek; sharp breaths of ''anger'' puff’d
Her fairy nostril out. ''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien.</poem>}}
{{smaller block|One who fails in some simple mechanical action feels ''vexation'' at his own inability — a ''vexation'' arising quite apart from any importance of the end missed.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Psychol., § 517.}}}}
{{smaller block|Burning with ''indignation'', and rendered sullen by despair, . . . they refused to ask their lives at the hands of an insulting foe, and preferred death to submission.
{{right|''Irving'', Indian Character.}}}}
{{smaller block|When the injury he resented was a personal one, he apologized frankly for his ''anger'', if it had transgressed the bounds of Christian ''indignation''; but, when he was indignant with falsehood, injustice, or cowardly wrong done to another, it was terrible to see his whole face knit itself together with ''wrath''. {{right|''S. A. Brooke'', F. W. Robertson, II. ii.}}}}

readings.c1s4:
¶
{{smaller block|To be angry about trifles is mean and childish; to rage and be furious is brutish; and to maintain perpetual ''wrath'' is akin to the practice and temper of devils; but to prevent and suppress rising ''resentment'' is wise and glorious, is manly and divine.
{{right|''Watts.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Mad ''ire'', and wrathful ''fury'', makes me weep.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iv. 3.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|He’s rash, and very sudden in ''choler'', and haply may strike at you. {{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>For blind with ''rage'' she miss’d the plank, and roll’d
In the river.</poem>{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Beware the ''fury'' of a patient man.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Abs. and Achit., i. 1005.}}}}

'''anger<sup>1</sup>''' (ang′gėr), ''v.'' [< ME. ''angren'', ''angeren'', pain, trouble, vex, < Icel. ''angra'' = Sw. ''ångra'' = Dan. ''angre'', in similar sense; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To grieve; trouble; distress; afflict. —'''2†.''' To make painful; cause to smart; inflame; irritate: as, to ''anger'' an ulcer. ''Bacon.'' —'''3.''' To excite to anger or wrath; rouse resentment in.
{{smaller block|There were some late taxes and impositions introduced, which rather ''angered'' than grieved the people. {{right|''Clarendon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The lips of young orangs and chimpanzees are protruded, sometimes to a wonderful degree. . . . They act thus, not only when slightly ''angered'', sulky, or disappointed, but when alarmed at anything. {{right|''Darwin'', Express. of Emotions, p. 140.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' To irritate, chafe, provoke, vex, enrage, exasperate, infuriate.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become angry. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>When neebors ''anger'' at a plea,
And just as wud as wud can be,
How easy can the barley bree
Cement the quarrel!</poem>{{right|''Burns'', Scotch Drink.}}}}

'''anger<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' An occasional spelling of ''angor''.

'''angerly''' (ang′gėr-li), ''a.'' [< ''anger''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>; = Icel. ''angrligr'', sad. The adv. is much older: see ''angerly'', ''adv.''] Inclined to anger. ''Byron.''

